	abstract = {This study innovatively develops a machine learning framework for cross-market palm oil futures arbitrage, combining cointegration analysis with predictive modeling to address commodity market inefficiencies. Our notable breakthroughs include: (1) A hybrid methodology that amalgamates econometric cointegration testing with machine learning forecasts; (2) Dynamic trading signals derived from rolling volatility bands; and (3) Empirical data demonstrating machine learning's ability to elucidate complex interdependencies among agricultural commodities. Employing Support Vector Regression (SVR), Random Forest, and Decision Tree models to predict FCPO-P.DCE spread data, we illustrate that SVR achieves the highest annualized return rate of 38.62% and the strongest risk-adjusted performance with a Sharpe ratio of 1.23, while Decision Tree also delivers a positive annualized return of 15.44% and a Sharpe ratio of 0.40. Notable breakthroughs comprise: 1) A hybrid methodology that amalgamates econometric cointegration testing with machine learning forecasts; 2) Dynamic trading signals derived from rolling volatility bands; 3) Empirical data demonstrating machine learning's ability to elucidate interdependencies among agricultural commodities. The findings progress quantitative finance by demonstrating how machine learning improves traditional pairs trading via enhanced identification of high-dimensional market correlations, offering institutional investors a robust tool for commodity arbitrage while providing theoretical insights into market efficiency dynamics in emerging derivatives markets.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
