	abstract = {Predicting agricultural commodity prices is a critical challenge that significantly impacts stakeholders such as farmers, traders, and policymakers. Accurate predictions enable better decision-making regarding production, pricing, and resource allocation, underscoring the importance of reliable forecasting methods. This study aims to compare traditional statistical techniques, including ARIMA and SARIMAX, with advanced machine learning algorithms, specifically a multivariate Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model and LightGBM, in predicting the stock prices of U.S. wheat from 2021 to February 21, 2025. We use a robust dataset comprising historical stock prices, enhanced by calculating statistical measures from lagged values to capture both short-term (1-day) and longer trends (7 and 30 days). Our modeling results reveal that the multivariate LSTM exhibited the best performance, with a Mean Squared Error (MSE) of 0.0015 and a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 0.0389, outperforming LightGBM and a tuned SARIMAX model. The superior performance of the LSTM model is attributed to its capability of capturing complex temporal dependencies and nonlinear patterns prevalent in financial time series data. This study emphasizes the potential of machine learning in enhancing predictive accuracy in agricultural commodities, ultimately assisting stakeholders in navigating market dynamics effectively.  © 2025 IEEE.},
