	abstract = {The prices of agricultural commodities in Bangladesh are shift highly just because of market and climate insecurity, making challenges for farmers, buyer and seller, and policymakers. Machine Learning (ML) Traditional models mostly lose to catch the non-linear, long-range patterns driving these up and down, guiding to unbelievable predictions and economic vulnerability across the supply chain. To fix this issue, this research introduces a deep learning (DL) framework based model on Gated Recurrent Units (GRU) for short-term forecasting of monthly closing prices of agriculture products using an compact dataset of bd market and weather attributes created from the World Bank and Bangladesh Agricultural Research Council (BARC) records. We collect the data from the years of 2007 to 2023 and this dataset is prepared through a design pipeline involving normalization, encoding, sequence generation and a strict chronological split (70% training, 10% validation, and 20% testing) to save data leakage. The proposed GRU model learns patterns over time and produces accurate predictions, achieving strong and genuine forecasting performance, with a coefficient of determination (R<sup>2</sup>) of 0.977, Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) of 6.00, Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 3.96, and Mean Squared Error (MSE) of 36.02. We check the model using convergence and residual plots to make sure the model's performance well and fair error distribution. The results show that the GRU model gives a strong base for useful tools such as, price alerts, buying plans and market risk control, helping stakeholders manage agricultural product price volatility. © 2026 IEEE.},
