	abstract = {Accurate agricultural commodity price estimation is essential for farmers, merchants, and policymakers to make educated decisions. This study explores the application of machine learning (ML) approaches for commodity price forecasting, including ensemble methods, support vector machines, artificial neural networks, and decision tree classifiers. The study also assesses how ML techniques can improve forecast accuracy when combined with conventional time series models. The data set used in this analysis was obtained from government platforms and research institutes and covers the years 2001-2024. It takes into account past price data, weather trends, market sentiment, and sensor data, such as soil moisture, temperature, and humidity readings. A deeper comprehension of current environmental elements influencing farm pricing is made possible by the integration of sensor data. In addition, issues like data quality issues and outside factors like policy modifications and world events are covered. The findings indicate that machine learning techniques, particularly when paired with sensor data, can greatly increase forecast accuracy. However, further work is needed to resolve limits and enhance models for more dependability. The results can also be used to increase supply chain and storage efficiency and optimize crop storage systems, including onions.  © 2025 IEEE.},
