	abstract = {Accurate agricultural commodity price forecasting plays a crucial role in aiding farmers, policymakers, and market stakeholders to make informed decisions. Using past meteorological data and market trends, this research offers a machine learningbased method for predicting agricultural commodity prices. The model makes use of the potent gradient boosting method XGBoost, which is renowned for its accuracy and resilience. Gathering historical commodity pricing data and pertinent environmental variables including temperature, precipitation, humidity, and sun radiation is the first step in the workflow. To guarantee data quality and consistency, preprocessing methods such as imputation, encoding, and normalizing were used. In order to capture patterns such as moving averages and seasonality, feature engineering was done. To assess model performance, the dataset was divided into training and testing sets. The trained model has a mean absolute error of 172.45 units, a high R2 score of 0.91, and an accuracy of 88.79%. These findings suggest a high capacity for prediction. Users can engage with the projections by viewing the final predictions on an intuitive dashboard. In the agricultural sector, this approach can be used as a decision support tool to help with more sustainable resource allocation and planning. Adding real-time data streams and broadening the scope of commodities covered are potential future enhancements. © 2025 IEEE.},
