	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105024224159?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Fungicides play a crucial role in crop protection and food security by minimizing yield losses due to fungal diseases. However, their persistent residues in agricultural commodities have raised growing concerns for human health, environmental safety, and global trade. The establishment of Maximum Residue Limits (MRLs) by international bodies such as the FAO/WHO and Codex Alimentarius serves as a regulatory benchmark, defining the highest permissible concentration of pesticide residues in food or feed to ensure consumer safety. Despite this, variations in MRL values among countries often create challenges in harmonizing trade regulations. Advances in analytical technologies, including Gas Chromatography–Mass Spectrometry (GC–MS), Liquid Chromatography–Tandem Mass Spectrometry (LC-MS/MS), and High-Resolution Mass Spectrometry (HRMS), have significantly improved sensitivity and specificity in multi-residue detection. Efficient extraction and purification methods, particularly Quick, Easy, Cheap, Effective, Rugged, and Safe (QuEChERS) and Solid Phase Extraction (SPE), have enhanced recovery and reduced matrix interferences in complex agricultural samples. Nonetheless, detecting residues below regulatory limits and ensuring consistency across analytical platforms remain persistent challenges. Recent studies highlight the potential of artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), and portable biosensors to revolutionize real-time monitoring and predictive modelling of residue behavior. Future research should emphasize the development of validated, cost-effective multi-residue methods and promote harmonized global MRL frameworks to support sustainable agriculture, food safety, and international compliance. © 2024},
